  {
   "id": "D3",
   "subject": "divergence",
   "closes": "open finding F1",
   "as_received": "F1 -> D3. A declared, controlled refusal with a stable diagnostic. Use a constant bound checked before decoding (the reviewer suggests 2^28 bytes on a long-name or pax block), with the unit stated. Answer with the closed \"unreadable archive or manifest\" / E_ADAPTER_INPUT, and add named vectors on both sides of the bound in both engines. Record it in port-rulings.golden and port-notes.",
   "left_to_the_implementer": "The constant. It is the one the reviewer suggested: 2**28.",
   "bounds": [
    "The limit is METADATA_LIMIT = 2**28 = 268,435,456. Unit: bytes, as the size field of a tar header of type L (GNU long name), K (GNU long link), x (pax extended), g (pax global) or X (Solaris extended) states them, before tarfile rounds the count up to whole 512-byte blocks. A header that states exactly the limit is read; one byte more is refused.",
    "D3 covers an archive in whose listing tarfile comes to read the blocks of such a header that states more than the limit, and nothing else. The port refuses at the point where the oracle makes that read, before reading the blocks, with an OSError whose message is the constant \"tar metadata block above 268435456 bytes\"; archive_manifest answers \"unreadable archive or manifest\", code E_ADAPTER_INPUT.",
    "What the oracle refuses about the header before that read comes first in the port as well (a negative size, the frame limit). What the oracle answers from the read on is replaced. That answer can be: acceptance (the blocks are there; or they are no pax records and the header is not the first member, which ends a listing silently, O1.tar-listing); the same closed refusal (the data is missing: ReadError); MemoryError or OverflowError, which no handler of the engine names (a size the machine cannot allocate). On the second kind the public outcome does not change.",
    "A header of any other type may state any size. Every string the reader builds from a block at or below the limit has at most 2**28 UTF-16 units, below the longest string of both verified runtimes (2**29 - 24); the reader refuses to run where that does not hold.",
    "Memory is not part of D3 and is not ruled: open finding F4. At the limit either engine needs a few times the block (measured: oracle 0.8 to 1.0 GiB, port 0.9 to 1.0 GiB). A block built to be costly, or many blocks at the limit, need more than the heap of the runtime gives the port."
   ],
   "evidence": [
    "test/vectors/port-large.golden (cases metadata.*: at the limit and one byte above it, for a long name and for a pax header, both engines)",
    "test/differential/port_large.py",
    "test/vectors/port-divergences.golden (entries tar-metadata.*, one per header type)",
    "test/differential/port_primitives.py (kind tar, `metadata_limit`)",
    "test/unit/port-tar-limits.test.js"
   ]
  },
