  {
   "id": "L1",
   "subject": "review finding",
   "as_received": "Review L1. Close the second-pass gap: have `scan` record per-step (length, crc32) and have GzipSource fail closed on a mismatch, or argue why that is not worth it and narrow the wording. Your call. L2 (raw crashes under engine change) can stay as a B9 note.",
   "left_to_the_implementer": "Close the gap or narrow the wording. Chosen: close it.",
   "bounds": [
    "The checking pass records, for every piece of output, its length and the CRC-32 of its member up to and including it: eight bytes per step. Every later pass over the container (the listing, a backward seek, a member read) is held to that record piece by piece, and a member must end after the same number of steps at the same byte of the file.",
    "A later pass that differs ends in `UnsupportedRuntime`, as does damage reported in a later pass: the archive has passed its check by then, so the fault is the engine's and is not answered as \"unreadable archive\".",
    "What the record does not cover is a fault that changes a piece and leaves both its length and the running CRC-32 as they were.",
    "L2 stays as it was ruled, a note on B9: an engine whose errors arrive only asynchronously would end in an unhandled `error` event, a crash and not a refusal."
   ],
   "evidence": [
    "src/pylike/tarfile.ts (StepRecord, GzipSource)",
    "test/unit/port-runtime.test.js (faults injected into the stepwise call)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "D4",
   "subject": "divergence",
   "builds": "open finding F4",
   "as_received": "Goal: build F4 as declared divergence D4. Use controlled admission budgets, checked before the allocation they bound, so that the port answers with a stable typed refusal instead of a heap abort. Your proposed numbers are only starting candidates. Soma (technical owner) wants them calibrated from evidence, not asserted.",
   "as_received_points_of_the_technical_owner": [
    "1. Byte accounting. Unknown pax values may stay out of the byte budget only if you show they are stream-skipped: no large decode, buffer, or backing string retained through a slice. Otherwise count them, or prove a separate transient bound. Counting all pax metadata bytes read is an acceptable, conservative D4 contract. Choose one and justify it. Don't cover the gap with the record counter.",
    "2. Upper bound. \">40 bytes/number at an abort\" is a lower estimate, not a basis for a guarantee. Measure completed sparse and member costs on both Node versions, plus combined cases with several budgets near their limits at once, and derive the bound from those measurements. \"Packages don't need sparse\" doesn't replace a contract.",
    "3. Sparse units. GNU 1.0's declared count is pairs (regions), and your budget is in numbers. Check 2xcount without overflow. Give 0.0, 0.1, 1.0 and old GNU the same semantics, with no hidden factor of two.",
    "4. Heap precondition. A 2 GiB heap_size_limit is a candidate too, not a RAM guarantee. It doesn't reserve physical or cgroup memory, doesn't cover Buffer and native allocations, and says nothing about the heap being empty. State an honest runtime contract with measured headroom, and check it fits the target CLI. The role must not silently raise the heap to make a gate green."
   ],
   "as_received_from_the_delta_review": [
    "P0: make the byte budget the size field of every L/K/x/g/X header read, cumulatively. D3 becomes its single-block case, and this settles Soma's point 1 conservatively unless you prove stream-skip.",
    "P1: a GNU.sparse.map in a global header is re-split for every later extended header. A 3.3 KB archive aborts both Node versions (probes/global-sparse.py). Charge the sparse budget at every 0.1 expansion, and charge 0.0 ranges as they are collected.",
    "P2: the default heap follows the unit's MemoryMax (heap-limit-cgroup.log). Make an explicit --max-old-space-size=H primary. Size H from one combined at-budget vector (every budget at default-1, one D3-limit block, global sparse expansion, members) run under exactly H on both Node versions. The reader checks heap_size_limit >= H before parsing. State the off-heap peak, so the role can set MemoryMax >= H + off-heap.",
    "Form 1.0: the D4 vectors must classify damaged-count cases, or charge per number as you append."
   ],
   "as_received_acceptance": "Acceptance: sparse-map amplification and many large names, below, at and above each bound, on both Node versions; a controlled refusal with measured memory; CI vectors with reduced test budgets; a test that the production defaults are really applied; the supported corpus still verified unchanged; new refusals recorded as D4; proposed defaults with their evidence, for Soma to approve. F4 and port-ready stay red until a delta review of this round.",
   "transcription": "The brief writes two signs this file does not hold, being ASCII: a multiplication sign in point 3 (here \"x\") and, in P2, \"default\" followed by a minus sign and 1 and two \"greater than or equal\" signs (here \"default-1\" and \">=\"). Nothing else is changed.",
   "earlier_lines_of_the_technical_owner": "Received through the manager while the second fix round was being closed, and quoted in that round's report (sha256 03d30d58bb86a1625cba4fd42a198a6decfeb8168a804ca9cc018ce09a3ff274): \"For the proposal, she wants the next round to have a deterministic contract, not \\\"free RAM\\\": a cumulative budget over held names/pax bytes and over record and field counts across the whole archive; a separate bound on structural expansion, e.g. the number count in GNU.sparse.map; the check runs before any large split, array or accumulation, with a stable typed error that doesn't echo the huge value; documented units and defaults, with the defaults justified by you. A budget refusal where the oracle accepts is a new declared divergence or an explicit D3 extension, not D1.\"",
   "left_to_the_implementer": "The numbers, as a proposal for the technical owner to approve (`defaults`). Three choices of unit and place that the brief left open or gave as alternatives, each stated under `choices`.",
   "defaults": {
    "status": "proposed, not approved",
    "budgets": {
     "metadataBytes": 268435456,
     "paxRecords": 16777216,
     "sparseNumbers": 1048576,
     "members": 131072
    },
    "heap_limit_required_mib": 3072,
    "what_the_heap_number_is": "A bound on `v8.getHeapStatistics().heap_size_limit`, which is the old space `--max-old-space-size` sets plus the young generation. It is not the flag: on the development host a limit of exactly 3,072 MiB is `--max-old-space-size=3024` on Node 22.23.3 and `=2880` on 24.14.0, and inside a cgroup of 2 GiB the same flags give 3,048 and 2,976 MiB. The reader checks the limit itself, at run time. The measurements ran at a limit of exactly 3,072 MiB.",
    "measured_at_that_limit": "The costliest archive found within the budgets, Node 22.23.3 / 24.14.0. One listing: 1,237 / 1,236 MiB of heap in use at the peak (1,234 to 1,239 across runs), 301 / 335 MiB of Buffers, 1,616 / 1,700 MiB resident. Three listings one after another in one process: 1,952 / 1,950 MiB in use, 2,350 / 2,487 MiB resident; completes under MemoryMax=2560M. The same with 524 MiB of caller data on the heap: 2,475 MiB in use, 2,537 / 2,610 MiB committed, inside the limit, 2,869 / 3,012 MiB resident; completes under MemoryMax=3072M and is killed under 2560M. With 1,050 MiB of caller data: 3,000 MiB in use, the limit reached (3,136 MiB committed on 24.14.0), completes. With 1,575 MiB: the process aborts.",
    "margin": "1,120 MiB between the peak of consecutive listings (1,952 MiB) and the limit: 36% of the heap. The first number proposed, 2,048 MiB, left none: at that limit three consecutive listings had 1,950 MiB in use and 2,085 MiB committed on Node 24.14.0, and with 524 MiB of caller data the process aborted.",
    "environment_requirement": "A V8 heap limit of at least 3,072 MiB, and memory for what the process comes to use: up to the heap limit, 301 to 377 MiB of Buffers, about 60 MiB for the runtime, and the archive file. Target memory is not known: kitchen has no deployed target and no unit that runs it. This is a requirement on the environment, not a statement that any host meets it. The role was not changed.",
    "evidence": "docs/port-notes.md, \"The admission budgets\": cost of each budget on Node 22.23.3 and 24.14.0, the survey of 323 package archives, the two combined vectors at exactly this heap limit, consecutive listings with caller data, and what was measured inside cgroup scopes."
   },
   "choices": [
    "Byte accounting (point 1, P0): every metadata byte read is counted, kept or not. The unit is the size field of every L, K, x, g and X header whose blocks are read, plus 512 for every block read for a sparse map (the data blocks of form 1.0, old GNU extension blocks). The second part goes beyond P0: the numbers of form 1.0 are member data, up to about a thousand digits each, and no header's size field covers them.",
    "Sparse unit (point 3): numbers, in all four forms; a region is two. The count a 1.0 map states is a count of regions and is never compared with the budget: each number is counted as it is read, so 2 x count is not computed for the budget at all, and the loop bound is exact at any size. This is the second alternative the delta review gave for form 1.0.",
    "Sparse 0.0 (P1): the offset and numbytes records are no longer collected while the header is parsed; they are read from the block a second time when form 0.0 applies, and counted as they are converted. Nothing is collected before it is counted, which is what \"charge 0.0 ranges as they are collected\" asks for, by another route.",
    "The refusal stands where the oracle's own count passes the budget. A GNU.sparse.map is not split: it is read number by number, so an invalid number before that point is tarfile's ValueError as before, and one behind it is never looked at.",
    "The heap limit required: 3,072 MiB, after 2,048 was measured to leave no margin for archives listed one after another (ruling D4, `defaults`)."
   ],
   "bounds": [
    "Four budgets, each a count over one listing (one tarfile.open and getmembers): metadataBytes 2**28 = 268,435,456; paxRecords 2**24 = 16,777,216; sparseNumbers 2**20 = 1,048,576; members 2**17 = 131,072. A listing that ends exactly at a budget is read; one unit more is refused. Nothing is credited back.",
    "The refusal is an OSError with the constant message \"tar metadata budget exceeded: \" followed by \"metadata bytes\", \"pax records\", \"sparse numbers\" or \"members\"; it holds no number and nothing from the archive. archive_manifest answers \"unreadable archive or manifest\", code E_ADAPTER_INPUT.",
    "D4 covers an archive in whose listing tarfile comes to pass one of the four counts, counted as test/differential/tar_budget.py counts them on the oracle's own tarfile, and nothing else. The port refuses at that point. What the oracle refuses before that point comes first in the port as well; what it answers from there on is replaced: acceptance, the same closed refusal (the public outcome does not change), or a class no handler of the engine names.",
    "D3 is the case of one block and keeps its own refusal and vectors: a block above 2**28 bytes is refused as before, whatever the budget.",
    "The budgets are sized for a V8 heap limit (`heap_size_limit`) of at least 3,072 MiB (HEAP_LIMIT_REQUIRED_MIB). Under a lower limit a listing ends in UnsupportedRuntime before anything is parsed: a plain Error, not an archive refusal, without a public code (backlog B10). Measured at exactly that limit on the costliest archive found within the budgets: 1,239 MiB of heap in use at the peak of one listing, 1,952 MiB at the peak of listings that follow one another in one process.",
    "What the heap requirement does not promise, each measured: the limit reserves no memory (a process is killed by its cgroup below it); it does not count Buffers or the compressed file (301 to 377 MiB of Buffers at the peak, plus the archive); V8 passes it when the heap is nearly full (3,136 MiB committed under a limit of 3,072 on Node 24.14.0, with 1,050 MiB of caller data); and what the caller holds on the heap comes out of the same limit.",
    "What is shared between members, and why that is no copy: the integer of a size in a global header and the text of a name in a global header. Both are immutable values of the runtime; a member holds a reference, and a name with trailing slashes stripped is a view of the one text. test/unit/port-tar-budgets.test.js measures both: 20,000 members under a size of 4,300 digits, 2,000 members under a path of 4 MiB. A sparse list is not shared: every expansion makes its own and is counted.",
    "Not bounded by D4: time. An archive within all four budgets can take hours in both engines (a name of 2**28 bytes in a global header, applied to every member); backlog B12."
   ],
   "evidence": [
    "src/pylike/tarfile.ts (Budgets, BUDGETS, HEAP_LIMIT_REQUIRED_MIB, MetadataBudget, charge)",
    "test/differential/tar_budget.py (the oracle counted at the same points)",
    "test/differential/port_budget.py and test/vectors/port-budget.golden (each budget from three sides at its production value, the four sparse forms, two combined vectors; the port under exactly --max-old-space-size=2048)",
    "test/differential/port_primitives.py (kind tarbudget: 12,000 archives under small budgets)",
    "test/unit/port-tar-budgets.test.js",
    "test/vectors/port-divergences.golden (entries tar-budget.*)",
    "test/mutants/reader_mutants.py and test/mutants/reader-results.golden"
   ],
   "as_received_during_the_round": [
    {
     "at": "2026-11-15T09:16:48Z",
     "from": "the technical owner, through the manager",
     "text": "Soma's clarifications for round 3:\n1. P1: D4's budget must cover the repeated per-member expansion and retention of structures, not only the numbers read once from the global pax. If you share one immutable structure across members instead, prove the semantics and that no hidden copies happen. P0 alone does not close P1.\n2. An explicit heap (--max-old-space-size) is allowed for calibration in your tests only. Do not change the production role or MemoryMax. The defaults need a combined peak, the off-heap peak and an honest margin.\n3. Keep reader (tarfile.ts) mutants as their own denominator, separate from 262/270. A surviving fault gets either a controlled witness or an honest, argued classification, never a formal PASS."
    },
    {
     "at": "2026-11-15T10:15:16Z",
     "from": "the technical owner, through the manager",
     "text": "From Soma, for your final evidence (part of her earlier requirement, not new scope): \"peak just under H\" is not headroom. State exactly what H is (the old-space flag vs the resulting heap_size_limit), and show measured heap and RSS peaks, the cgroup MemoryMax used, and the remaining margin with caller data included. With target RAM unknown, state an environment requirement only. Don't claim compatibility with any host, and don't touch the role."
    },
    {
     "at": "2026-11-15T10:19:57Z",
     "from": "the manager",
     "text": "Scope narrowing; everyone agrees on this direction (owner poll pending on the details). Kitchen's inputs are our own archives, digest-pinned and checked before reading, so the threat model is trusted and hash-verified inputs. Finish this round as a conservative first-version contract:\n- choose limits with clear margin, generous for the real package corpus you surveyed;\n- refuse everything above them with the typed D4 refusal;\n- verify the supported corpus is unchanged.\n\nDon't search for further amplification routes, and don't maximise what can be accepted. List any route you know of and haven't closed as backlog in the report, unless it bypasses the declared limits, in which case close it. A crash inside the declared range must still not ship. Then commit, run the gates and finalize."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "C1",
   "subject": "precondition",
   "as_received": "Owner decision (Vasyl, poll 2296): finish this round with the conservative first-version contract already sent. One narrow delta review follows. New routes go to backlog, then task B and the catalog. Record in the rulings that the trusted-source, digest-checked-before-parse input is a caller precondition, to be confirmed at catalog integration, not a proven property. If F4 is left as residual risk of the trusted-input version, record it as that, changing the readiness criterion honestly, not as fixed or PASS.",
   "bounds": [
    "The input of the archive reader is taken to come from a trusted source and to be checked against its pinned digest before it is parsed. That is a precondition on the caller. It is to be confirmed at catalog integration and is not a proven property.",
    "What archive_manifest itself does: it compares the sha256 of the bytes it read with the digest its entry declares before the tar reader is called (archive.py:126-127, row 39). Where the entry and its digest come from, and whether that source is trusted, is the caller's.",
    "D4 is the first version of a contract for such input: limits with a clear margin above the package archives surveyed, a typed refusal above them, the supported corpus unchanged. It does not claim to bound what an archive built to be costly can do. Further amplification routes were not searched for after this decision; the ones known and not closed are in the backlog (B12, B13, B15).",
    "What D4 leaves of finding F4 is residual risk of this trusted-input version and is recorded as such under the finding. It is not recorded as fixed."
   ],
   "evidence": [
    "test/port-rulings.golden (obligation archive.trusted-input, finding F4)",
    "docs/port-notes.md (\"The admission budgets\")"
   ]
  }
 ],
