 "open_findings": [
  {
   "id": "F1",
   "found": "by the implementer in the fix round, while measuring what the archive reader still holds in memory",
   "status": "ruled",
   "finding": "A tar name or a pax value longer than 536,870,888 UTF-16 units (the longest string of the JavaScript runtime) ends the port in a RangeError of the runtime, where the oracle accepts the archive. Measured with a pax value and a GNU long name of 600 MiB: the oracle accepts at 2.4 GiB and 1.8 GiB peak, the port fails closed at 1.2 GiB. At 300 MiB both accept. Present at ac5e89c as well; the fix round did not change it.",
   "asks": "Ruled in the second fix round: a declared refusal, divergence D3.",
   "backlog": "B8",
   "ruling": "D3"
  },
  {
   "id": "F4",
   "found": "by the implementer in the second fix round, while checking that the limit of D3 is enough on its own",
   "status": "open: built as divergence D4 in the third fix round; what remains is residual risk of the trusted-input version (ruling C1), not fixed; the budgets and the heap requirement are proposed and not approved, and that round has no delta review yet",
   "finding": "Valid archives at or below the metadata limit of D3 on which the oracle answers and the port does not: the port runs out of heap and the process aborts (\"JavaScript heap out of memory\", exit by SIGABRT, no receipt). The heap limit is the runtime's, about 4 GiB by default on Node 22.23.3 and 24.14.0, less on a small machine, more with --max-old-space-size; it can come before the memory of the machine does. The oracle has no such ceiling: it ends in an uncaught MemoryError only when the machine has no more. It fails closed. No limit on a single block removes it.",
   "measured": [
    "One pax header of 268,435,417 bytes (below the limit) that holds one GNU.sparse.map of 2**27 numbers: the oracle accepts in 9 s at 7.6 GiB peak; the port aborts after 29 s at 5.7 GiB resident.",
    "Sixteen members, each with a GNU long name of exactly 2**28 bytes (4 GiB of names): the oracle accepts in 5 s at 4.6 GiB peak; the port aborts after 31 s at 4.4 GiB resident. With eight such members both accept (oracle 2.6 GiB, port 4.2 GiB).",
    "Read by both engines at the limit: 53.6 million pax records of five bytes (oracle 27 s, port 7 s); 2**24 + 4,096 distinct pax keywords (oracle 8 s at 2.1 GiB, port 3 s at 0.3 GiB, after the fix F3); 11.6 million records of the sparse form 0.0 (oracle 7 s, port 4 s).",
    "In the delta review of the second fix round (P1): a GNU.sparse.map of 2**20 numbers in a global header and 80 members behind one-record extended headers, 3.3 KB compressed: the oracle accepts at 2.9 GiB; the port aborts at 4.1 GiB on Node 22.23.3 and 4.0 GiB on 24.14.0. With D4 the same archive is refused at the second expansion of the map, in 0.2 s, at 173 MiB resident on Node 22.23.3 and 153 MiB on 24.14.0."
   ],
   "asks": "Ruled in direction, twice. While the second fix round was being closed: keep it open and red, with the measurements and a written proposal, and build nothing then (the lines are quoted under ruling D4, `earlier_lines_of_the_technical_owner`). In the brief of the third fix round: \"build F4 as declared divergence D4\", with numbers \"calibrated from evidence, not asserted\", and \"F4 and port-ready stay red until a delta review of this round.\" By the owner's decision during that round (ruling C1) the round is finished as a conservative first version for trusted input, and what it leaves is recorded as residual risk. What is still asked: approval of the proposed defaults of ruling D4, and the one narrow delta review of the third fix round.",
   "backlog": "B11",
   "ruling": "D4",
   "residual_risk": [
    "The limits are proposed, not approved, and the memory they need is stated as a requirement on an environment that does not exist yet.",
    "The costliest archive within the budgets is the costliest that was built, not a proven maximum. After the owner's decision no further amplification routes were searched for.",
    "The V8 heap limit is not a hard bound. With more of the caller's data on the heap than was measured to fit (524 MiB inside the limit, 1,050 MiB at it), a listing within the budgets can still end the process: at 1,575 MiB it aborted.",
    "Time is not bounded (backlog B12): an archive within every budget can take hours, in both engines.",
    "All of this is accepted for input that is trusted and digest-checked before it is parsed. That is a precondition on the caller (ruling C1, obligation archive.trusted-input), to be confirmed at catalog integration."
   ]
  }
 ],
 "backlog": [
  {
   "id": "B1",
   "for": "oracle",
   "entry": "Refuse a recipe that is not UTF-8 in `verify_bytes`, in both engines, by a reviewed waiver."
  },
  {
   "id": "B2",
   "for": "oracle and plan",
   "entry": "Bound document depth explicitly, well below the interpreter limits, so that no refusal depends on the caller's depth."
  },
  {
   "id": "B3",
   "for": "plan",
   "entry": "Pin the oracle interpreter by the sha256 of its files (urllib/parse.py, tarfile.py, gzip.py, json/decoder.py), not by the name \"CPython 3.12.3\"."
  },
  {
   "id": "B4",
   "for": "oracle",
   "entry": "Refuse an archive whose tar listing ends on an invalid header instead of an end marker."
  },
  {
   "id": "B5",
   "for": "port, when cook and catalog are ported",
   "entry": "Decide whether the key order of the merged configuration is observable downstream. If it is, port the order rule of `merge_hash` (an equal upper mapping replaces the lower one)."
  },
  {
   "id": "B6",
   "for": "port",
   "entry": "`combine` in src/config.ts is the stand-in and matches Ansible only in the `merge_layers` shape. A second caller needs Ansible's semantics for three or more layers and for layers that are not mappings."
  },
  {
   "id": "B7",
   "for": "port, with cook or deploy",
   "entry": "The end-to-end witness for archive.L140.4 (obligation above)."
  },
  {
   "id": "B8",
   "for": "closed",
   "entry": "Open finding F1, ruled as divergence D3 in the second fix round."
  },
  {
   "id": "B9",
   "for": "port",
   "entry": "The stepwise gzip reader drives Node's zlib engine through the call `inflateRawSync` uses internally, on the Node versions of STEPWISE_RUNTIMES only (ruling B9). Replace it when Node documents a synchronous inflate that can be resumed. Until then two modes of a changed engine end as a crash and not as `UnsupportedRuntime` (review finding L2): an engine whose errors arrive only asynchronously leaves an unhandled `error` event, and a change the checks of the reader do not name surfaces as whatever the runtime throws."
  },
  {
   "id": "B10",
   "for": "port, with the command line (plan phase 7)",
   "entry": "Check the runtime once at start and report an unverified one as a missing prerequisite with a public code, before any archive is read. Today it surfaces at the first archive, as `UnsupportedRuntime` without a code."
  },
  {
   "id": "B11",
   "for": "approval and review",
   "entry": "Open finding F4, built as divergence D4 in the third fix round. Owed: the technical owner's approval of the proposed budgets and heap requirement, and the delta review of that round."
  },
  {
   "id": "B12",
   "for": "ruling",
   "entry": "Time is not bounded by D4. A name value in a global pax header is applied to every later member, and both engines look at the whole name for each member (the oracle in `rstrip` and `re.fullmatch`, the port in `rstripSlash` and the manifest-name test). Measured through archive_manifest with one path of 2**28 bytes in a global header, on a host that was doing other work: for a path that is a run of slashes about 0.5 s a member in the port and 0.8 s in the oracle; for one without a trailing slash about 0.1 s in either. At the member budget that is hours in both engines. One way to bound it: count a name inherited from a global header against the byte budget each time it is applied, as a global sparse map is counted each time it is expanded."
  },
  {
   "id": "B13",
   "for": "port",
   "entry": "`decodeUtf8` builds a long text in pieces and the first use flattens it, so a metadata block costs twice its text at the peak (up to 1,050 MiB of heap for a block of 2**28 bytes that are no UTF-8). Decoding into one flat string would take about 0.5 GiB off the peak of one listing; the heap requirement was sized with the cost as it is."
  },
  {
   "id": "B14",
   "for": "role, with the command line (plan phase 7)",
   "entry": "Whoever starts kitchen has to give it a V8 heap limit of at least 3,072 MiB (for example `--max-old-space-size=3072` on the command line of Node; the role refuses NODE_OPTIONS in a unit environment) and the memory ruling D4 states. The role was not changed in this round. The reader refuses to list under a lower limit, with `UnsupportedRuntime` and no public code (B10)."
  },
  {
   "id": "B15",
   "for": "ruling, at catalog integration",
   "entry": "Known and not closed, by the owner's decision for the trusted-input version (ruling C1): the compressed archive is held as a whole, in both engines, and its size is bounded by nothing here; what the caller holds on the heap comes out of the limit the reader requires; and no further amplification routes were searched for. If the precondition of C1 is dropped, these are the first to look at, with B12."
  }
 ],
 "fixed_without_ruling": [
  {
   "id": "F2",
   "found": "by the implementer in the second fix round, while checking that the limit of D3 is enough on its own",
   "finding": "Two patterns of the tar reader ended in `+$` and took time quadratic in a run that is not at the end: `rstrip(\"/\")` of a pax path and the blanks around a pax number. Measured at a198042: 6 to 9 s for a run of 160,000 characters, so hours for a few MiB, where the oracle takes milliseconds. Present at ac5e89c as well. No wrong answer, no answer.",
   "fix": "Both are one pass now (`rstripSlash`, `DecimalScanner`), and no more than 4,300 digits of a number are kept. The helper differential compares `int()` on 9,688 generated texts and the tar listing on 12,000 archives; test/unit/port-tar-limits.test.js bounds the time on runs of 2 MiB."
  },
  {
   "id": "F3",
   "found": "as F2",
   "finding": "A pax header with very many records ended the port where the oracle accepts the archive. The port kept every record of every header: two Buffers in a list and an entry in a Map, as the oracle keeps them in a dict; global headers accumulate over the archive. Measured with one header of 2**24 + 4,096 distinct keywords (176 MiB of records, below the limit of D3): the oracle accepts in 8 s at 2.1 GiB; the build of a198042 aborts with \"JavaScript heap out of memory\" at 4.5 GiB. A Map of the runtime also ends at 2**24 keys (\"RangeError: Map maximum size exceeded\"), which that build would have met next. Present at ac5e89c as well.",
   "fix": "Only the nine keywords the reader looks at are kept, in the order and with the values the dict would give them; no other record can reach a name, a size or an exception. The values of the two keywords the sparse form 0.0 reads from the raw records are kept as byte ranges. The same header now: accepted in 3 s at 0.3 GiB."
  },
  {
   "id": "F5",
   "found": "by the implementer in the third fix round: the first combined vector at the budgets ended in a runtime error instead of a listing",
   "finding": "The manifest-name test of `archive_manifest` was the pattern /^[^/]+\\/package[^\\n]json$/u. On a member name that is not Latin-1, V8 answers it with \"RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded\" from 8,387,585 UTF-16 units on (Node 22.23.3 and 24.14.0); on a Latin-1 name it answers up to 2**28. So an archive with one name of 8.4 million CJK characters, far below the limit of D3, ended the port in a runtime error no handler names, where the oracle accepts it. Present at ac5e89c as well.",
   "fix": "The test is spelled out in one pass, without a pattern (`MANIFEST_NAME` in src/archive.ts). test/unit/port-manifest-name.test.js holds it against the pattern on 600 generated names and on fixed ones, and reads names of nine million characters; its six mutants are in the reader mutant set."
  },
  {
   "id": "F6",
   "found": "by the implementer in the third fix round, while listing what a member can cost that no budget counts",
   "finding": "A `size`, `GNU.sparse.size` or `GNU.sparse.realsize` in a global pax header was converted anew for every later member. An integer of 4,300 digits is 1.8 kB, so 2**20 members under one global record of 4.3 kB were 1.9 GiB of integers. The oracle does the same and holds them.",
   "fix": "The integer of a global value is converted once and shared by the members it reaches (`converted` in the reader). The answers are the same; test/unit/port-tar-budgets.test.js holds the values and bounds the memory of 20,000 members under such a record."
  }
 ],
